{
  "requirement": "When implementation shows end-user-observable behavior no requirement covers, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to draft a requirement for it and take it through review, agreement, and commit before implementing that behavior.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.text_change.uncovered` says that when implementation shows end-user-observable behavior no requirement covers, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to draft a requirement for it. The requirement must then go through review, agreement, and commit before that behavior is implemented.\n\n**What the skill says.** In `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, the paragraph after the Workflow list ends with \"Behavior no requirement covers goes back to step 2.\" Step 2 is \"Draft.\" Steps 3 to 5 are Review, Present with the **Gate:**, and Commit. Step 6 is \"Implement and test.\"\n\n**Test mapping.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_drafts_a_requirement_for_uncovered_behavior]` carries the `{test}` reference to the subject. It makes two assertions:\n- `assert_phrases` checks that the bundled skill text contains \"Behavior no requirement covers goes back to step 2\". `assert_phrases` calls `assert_text`, which asserts `contains` for each phrase and panics with a message if one is missing.\n- `step(\"habeas-requirements\", 2).starts_with(\"Draft.\")` uses `workflow_steps`, which parses the numbered steps of the Workflow section. This checks that the step the behavior is routed to is the drafting step.\n\nTogether these cover the subject's distinctive obligation: uncovered behavior is routed back to drafting.\n\n**Review, agreement, and commit before implementation.** The test does not assert the ordering of steps 3 to 6. That ordering is the subject of sibling requirements, and each has its own marked test:\n- `requirements_review_skill.agreed_text`: `the_requirements_skill_reviews_every_change_to_agreed_text` checks that review is step 3 and that changes go through steps 3 to 5.\n- `requirements_review_skill.review` and `requirements_review_skill.present`: `the_requirements_skill_is_home_of_drafting_review_references_and_criteria` checks that the drafting, review, and **Gate:** steps exist.\n- `requirements_review_skill.commit`: `the_requirements_skill_commits_agreed_requirements_before_code` checks the commit-before-code order.\n\nThis is the same linear workflow that \"goes back to step 2\" re-enters, so those obligations are delegated to those requirements' tests. I did not read that last test's body. I rely on its name and its `SKILL` constant.\n\n**Possible weakness.** The assertion on the sentence is a plain substring match on the whole bundled skill. It does not check that the sentence is tied to the \"implementation shows\" condition. The sentence is distinctive, and a removal or rewording would fail the test. I find no plausible requirement-violating change to the skill that this test and the sibling tests would all accept.\n\nNo `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply, and none are needed.",
  "references": "767c376379863a98568619d095e2deb7fb3395ce58580a05833756852609335d",
